// #2895: `Double` was the one instantiation the #2523 marker guard waved
// through, and it was exactly the failure the guard exists to prevent.
//
// `marker_cmp_is_by_value` listed `CtDouble` alongside `Int` / `Bool` / `Char`
// / `String`, on the reasoning that those are the scalars the prelude
// registers marker impls for. A `Double` is not a tagged immediate the way an
// `Int` is, so the raw comparison an erased bound falls back to reads the box
// rather than the number. Measured on the committed seed and on a stage2 built
// from this branch, identical both ways:
//
//   bare[T: Eq](1.5, 1.5)    FALSE   <- two equal Doubles
//   lt[T: Ord](2.5, 1.5)     TRUE    <- and Ord is wrong in BOTH directions
//   gt[T: Ord](2.5, 1.5)     FALSE      at once
//   1.5 == 1.5  (concrete)   true    <- the contrast
//
// `Eq` is no longer part of this: ADR-0097 gave it a method (#2523), so
// `[T: Eq]` at `Double` dispatches through a real witness and answers `true`
// (fixtures/eq_bound_derive_test.vibe). `Ord` is still a MARKER, so the guard
// still fires for it -- and this file pins the MESSAGE, not just the refusal.

fn smaller[T: Ord](x: T, y: T) -> Bool {
  x < y
}

fn main() -> Unit allows Stdout {
  println(__to_string(smaller(2.5, 1.5)))
}
